{:edb/db-before-t 2713 :edb/db-after-t 2714 :edb/tx-data [[13194139536026 50 #inst "2026-10-09T13:09:02.163Z" 13194139536026 true] [17592186184117 1020 #edb/ref 17592186379888 13194139536026 true] [17592186184117 1020 #edb/ref 17592186379895 13194139536026 true] [17592186184117 1020 #edb/ref 17592186379902 13194139536026 true] [17592186184148 1020 #edb/ref 17592186379874 13194139536026 true] [17592186184148 1020 #edb/ref 17592186379881 13194139536026 true] [17592186379874 1017 #edb/ref 17592186379875 13194139536026 true] [17592186379874 1089 "q-139475" 13194139536026 true] [17592186379874 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139536026 true] [17592186379874 1100 "Find the acute angle between the vector $x$ and the subspace spanned by the linearly independent vectors ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2}},$ where\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139536026 true] [17592186379874 1113 #uuid "c22671a2-0398-56c8-a280-658f39299023" 13194139536026 true] [17592186379874 1145 "The orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace spanned by the columns of a matrix $A$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x=A({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}\\,{A}^{T}\\,x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ {a}_{1} & {a}_{2} \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}]\\,⟹\\,{A}^{T}=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get the following projection:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}]\\cdot {([\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}])}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}]\\cdot {[\\begin{aligned}5 & 8 \\\\ 8 & 14\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{6}[\\begin{aligned}14 & -8 \\\\ -8 & 5\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe acute angle between $x$ and $S$ is the acute angle between $x$ and ${\\text{proj}}_{S}x.$ Equivalently, it is the acute angle between $x$ and any non-zero vector that is parallel to ${\\text{proj}}_{S}x.$ So, to make calculations easier, we can find the angle between the vectors\n\n$\\,$ $x=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,2\\,{\\text{proj}}_{S}x=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].$\n\nComputing the cosine using the dot product, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{(-1)\\cdot (-3)+(-1)\\cdot 0+2\\cdot 3}{\\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+(-1)^{2}+{2}^{2}}\\sqrt{(-3)^{2}+{0}^{2}+{3}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{\\sqrt{6}\\sqrt{18}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})=\\frac{\\pi }{6},\n$$\n\nwhich is already an acute angle." 13194139536026 true] [17592186379875 1000 #edb/ref 17592186379876 13194139536026 true] [17592186379875 1000 #edb/ref 17592186379877 13194139536026 true] [17592186379875 1000 #edb/ref 17592186379878 13194139536026 true] [17592186379875 1000 #edb/ref 17592186379879 13194139536026 true] [17592186379875 1000 #edb/ref 17592186379880 13194139536026 true] [17592186379875 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139536026 true] [17592186379875 1041 "selection" 13194139536026 true] [17592186379875 1104 #edb/ref 17592186379880 13194139536026 true] [17592186379875 1110 #uuid "24849fe2-d7aa-516f-8ad3-daf9